2026-06-25 11:26:36,701 | INFO     | agent_framework  | trace=366cb0289a4ac3910871ce44aa2e065e span=a27fe81cea0a2a48 parent=- | {'role': 'tool', 'parts': [{'type': 'tool_call_response', 'id': '9dns71ts4', 'response': '{\n  "results": [\n    {\n      "source": "CBUAE_Circular_2024_BSE_047_AI_Governance",\n      "clause": "2.1.1",\n      "section": "CENTRAL BANK OF THE UNITED ARAB EMIRATES",\n      "effective_date": "",\n      "stale": false,\n      "score": 0.0328,\n      "text": "## CENTRAL BANK OF THE UNITED ARAB EMIRATES\\n\\nBanking Supervision &amp; Examination Department\\n\\n## SUPERVISORY CIRCULAR\\n\\n## No. 2024/BSE/047\\n\\n## Governance and Risk Management Requirements for Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning Systems in Licensed Financial Institutions\\n\\n| Circular Reference   | CBUAE/BSE/2024/047                                                                                 |\\n|----------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\\n| Issue Date           | 15 March 2024                                                                                      |\\n| Effective Date       | 1 July 2024                                                                                        |\\n| Addressed To         | Chief Executive Officers and Chief Risk Officers of all Licensed Financial Institutions in the UAE |\\n| Subject              | AI/ML Governance, Model Risk Management, and Consumer Protection Requirements                      |\\n| Supersedes           | CBUAE/BSE/2022/031 (Guidance on Automated Decision-Making)                                         |\\n| Compliance Deadline  | 31 December 2024 for Tier 1 institutions; 30 June 2025 for all others                              |\\n\\n## Article 1 - Purpose, Scope and Legal Basis\\n\\n1.1 This Circular is issued pursuant to the powers vested in the Central Bank of the UAE under Federal DecreeLaw No. 14 of 2018 regarding the Central Bank and Organisation of Financial Institutions and Activities, as amended, and the accompanying Executive Regulations. 1.2 This Circular establishes minimum standards for the governance, development, deployment, monitoring, and decommissioning of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) systems used by Licensed Financial Institutions (LFIs) in the UAE in connection with credit decisions, risk management, pricing of financial products, customer service, fraud detection, and any other material business function. 1.3 This Circular applies to all LFIs licensed and supervised by the Central Bank of the UAE, including but not limited to national banks, foreign bank branches, finance companies, exchange houses, and payment service providers, to the extent that they deploy AI or ML systems in material business processes. 1.4 For the purposes of this Circular, an AI/ML system is considered to be deployed in a \'material business process\' where its outputs directly or indirectly influence decisions that: (a) affect a customer\'s access to or terms of a financial product or service; (b) generate recommendations that are presented to employees or customers as a basis for financial action; or (c) are used to calculate, estimate, or propose prices, rates, fees, or other commercial terms for financial products.\\n\\nIMPORTANT: The pricing of credit facilities, including the use of AI-generated pricing recommendations for corporate loans, revolving facilities, or any structured credit product, is explicitly classified as a material business process under Article 1.4(c). All requirements in this Circular apply to such systems.\\n\\n## Article 2 - Governance Framework Requirements\\n\\n## 2.1 Board and Senior Management Accountability\\n\\n2.1.1 The Board of Directors of every LFI deploying AI/ML systems in material business processes must adopt and maintain a written AI Governance Policy. This policy must be reviewed and re-approved by the Board at least annually. 2.1.2 A designated member of senior management, at a level no lower than Chief Risk Officer or equivalent, must be appointed as the AI Risk Officer responsible for overseeing the implementation of this Circular. 2.1.3 The AI Governance Policy must address, at minimum: (a) the LFI\'s risk appetite for AI-related risks; (b) accountability structures for AI development and deployment; (c) human oversight requirements; (d) model\\n\\ninventory management; (e) consumer protection obligations; and (f) regulatory reporting obligations.\\n\\n## 2.2 AI Model Inventory\\n\\n2.2.1 Every LFI must maintain a centralised, up-to-date AI Model Inventory covering all AI/ML systems deployed in material business processes. The inventory must be made available to the Central Bank upon request.\\n\\n2.2.2 For each model in the inventory, the following minimum information must be recorded:\\n\\n- Model name, version, and unique identifier.\\n- Business function and processes in which the model is deployed.\\n- Date of initial deployment and date of most recent material update.\\n- Model owner (business function) and model developer (technology/data team).\\n- Validation status and date of last independent validation.\\n- Current monitoring status (active / under review / decommissioned).\\n- Risk classification (High / Medium / Low) as determined under Article 4.\\n\\n## Article 3 - Model Risk Management\\n\\n## 3.1 Model Development Standards\\n\\n3.1.1 All AI/ML models deployed in material business processes must be developed in accordance with sound model development practices, including but not limited to: clearly documented model objectives; appropriate training data governance; out-of-time and out-of-sample testing; and documentation of model limitations and known failure modes.\\n\\n3.1.2 Training data used for models that influence credit pricing or credit decisions must be sourced from reliable, auditable data systems. Data lineage must be documented from source to model input."\n    },\n    {\n      "source": "CBUAE_Circular_2024_BSE_047_AI_Governance",\n      "clause": "6.2",\n      "section": "6.2 Customer Transparency",\n      "effective_date": "",\n      "stale": false,\n      "score": 0.0323,\n      "text": "## 6.2 Customer Transparency\\n\\n6.2.1 Where the terms offered to a customer (including pricing) have been materially influenced by an AI/ML system, and a customer requests an explanation of how their terms were determined, the LFI must be able to provide a meaningful explanation of the factors that influenced the outcome. The LFI is not required to disclose proprietary model architecture or training data.\\n\\n## Article 7 - Data Governance for AI Systems\\n\\n## 7.1 Data Quality\\n\\n7.1.1 LFIs must implement data quality controls to ensure that data used to train, validate, and operate AI/ML systems is accurate, complete, timely, and fit for purpose. Data quality assessments must be documented and reviewed at least annually. 7.1.2 For AI systems used in credit pricing, the following data governance requirements apply in addition to the\\n\\ngeneral requirements in Article 7.1.1:\\n\\n- Interest rate and FTP data used as inputs must be sourced directly from authorised internal Treasury systems and must not be manually overridden without a documented audit trail.\\n- Client risk rating data used as inputs must be sourced from the LFI\'s credit systems and must reflect the most recently approved internal credit rating.\\n- Market benchmark data (e.g., peer pricing) used as inputs must be sourced from identified, approved data providers and must be date-stamped.\\n\\n## 7.2 Data Retention and Lineage\\n\\n7.2.1 Complete data lineage - from source system to model input to model output - must be documented and retained for all HIGH-risk AI/ML system predictions. This lineage must support reconstruction of any recommendation for audit or regulatory review purposes.\\n\\n## Article 8 - Regulatory Reporting and Supervisory Access\\n\\n8.1 LFIs must submit an annual AI/ML Systems Return to the Central Bank of the UAE by 31 March each year, covering the preceding calendar year. The format of the Return will be prescribed by the Central Bank. 8.2 In addition to the annual Return, LFIs must notify the Central Bank within 30 calendar days of: (a) initial deployment of any new HIGH-risk AI/ML system; (b) any material modification to an existing HIGH-risk system; (c) any AI-related incident that results in material consumer detriment or regulatory breach. 8.3 The Central Bank reserves the right to conduct on-site or off-site examinations of LFIs\' AI/ML systems, governance frameworks, model documentation, and validation records at any time. LFIs must provide full\\n\\ncooperation with such examinations.\\n\\nNON-COMPLIANCE: LFIs that fail to comply with the requirements of this Circular by the applicable deadline will be subject to supervisory action, which may include direction to suspend the use of non-compliant AI/ML systems, imposition of administrative penalties, or other measures as provided under Federal Decree-Law No. 14 of 2018.\\n\\n## Article 9 - Transitional Provisions\\n\\n9.1 LFIs that are currently operating AI/ML systems in material business processes as of the issue date of this Circular must complete a gap assessment against the requirements herein within 60 days of the effective date (1 July 2024) and submit a remediation plan to the Central Bank. 9.2 Existing HIGH-risk systems that have not undergone independent model validation must complete validation\\n\\nby 31 December 2024 for Tier 1 institutions, or by 30 June 2025 for all other LFIs.\\n\\nIssued under the authority of the Central Bank of the UAE Banking Supervision &amp; Examination Department  |  Abu Dhabi, March 2024"\n    }\n  ],\n  "total_results": 2,\n  "freshness_warning": false,\n  "latency_ms": 914.79\n}'}]}
2026-06-25 11:26:41,710 | INFO     | agent_framework  | trace=366cb0289a4ac3910871ce44aa2e065e span=a27fe81cea0a2a48 parent=- | {'role': 'assistant', 'parts': [{'type': 'text', 'content': 'Based on the **Central Bank of the United Arab Emirates (CBUAE) Supervisory Circular No. 2024/BSE/047 (issued 15 March 2024)**, Licensed Financial Institutions (LFIs) must adhere to the following AI and Machine Learning (ML) governance requirements:\n\n### 1. Governance and Accountability\n*   **AI Governance Policy:** The Board of Directors must adopt and maintain a written AI Governance Policy, which must be reviewed and re-approved at least annually. [CBUAE_Circular_2024_BSE_047_AI_Governance · 2.1.1]\n*   **Senior Management Oversight:** A designated senior executive (at the level of Chief Risk Officer or above) must be appointed as the "AI Risk Officer" to oversee implementation. [CBUAE_Circular_2024_BSE_047_AI_Governance · 2.1.2] \n*   **Policy Content:** The policy must explicitly address the institution\'s risk appetite, accountability structures, human oversight requirements, and consumer protection obligations. [CBUAE_Circular_2024_BSE_047_AI_Governance · 2.1.3]\n\n### 2. Centralised AI Model Inventory\n*   Every LFI must maintain a centralised, up-to-date inventory of all AI/ML systems used in material business processes. [CBUAE_Circular_2024_BSE_047_AI_Governance · 2.2.1]\n*   The inventory must record the model\'s unique identifier, business function, deployment dates, owner/developer, last validation date, and its assigned risk classification (High, Medium, or Low). [CBUAE_Circular_2024_BSE_047_AI_Governance · 2.2.2]\n\n### 3. Model Risk Management & Development\n*   **Development Standards:** All models must be developed using sound practices, including documented objectives, out-of-time/sample testing, and a clear record of model limitations and known failure modes. [CBUAE_Circular_2024_BSE_047_AI_Governance · 3.1.1]\n*   **Credit Data Governance:** Models influencing credit or pricing must use data sourced from reliable, auditable systems with fully documented data lineage from source to input. [CBUAE_Circular_2024_BSE_047_AI_Governance · 3.1.2]\n\n### 4. Data Quality and Lineage\n*   Institutions must implement data quality controls (ensuring accuracy, completeness, and timeliness), with assessments documented and reviewed at least annually. [CBUAE_Circular_2024_BSE_047_AI_Governance · 7.1.1]\n*   For **HIGH-risk** AI systems, complete data lineage from the source system through to the model output must be retained to support audit and regulatory review. [CBUAE_Circular_2024_BSE_047_AI_Governance · 7.2.1]\n\n### 5. Consumer Protection and Transparency\n*   If a customer requests an explanation for terms (such as pricing) materially influenced by an AI system, the LFI must provide a meaningful explanation'}]}
