{"ts": "2026-06-26T11:38:55+0530", "level": "INFO", "logger": "agent_framework", "trace_id": "6171ac96850ea8387bcf401d58a12012", "span_id": "1e7de0b98d7a52d0", "parent_span_id": "-", "request_id": "981D9CEA", "msg": "{'role': 'tool', 'parts': [{'type': 'tool_call_response', 'id': '71nk45w7h', 'response': '{\\n  \"results\": [\\n    {\\n      \"source\": \"FAB_Model_Risk_Management_Framework_v3.1\",\\n      \"clause\": \"Section 2\",\\n      \"section\": \"FIRST ABU DHABI BANK\",\\n      \"effective_date\": \"\",\\n      \"stale\": false,\\n      \"score\": 0.0328,\\n      \"text\": \"## FIRST ABU DHABI BANK\\\\n\\\\nGroup Risk Management\\\\n\\\\n## MODEL RISK MANAGEMENT FRAMEWORK\\\\n\\\\nAI / ML and Quantitative Model Governance Standards\\\\n\\\\n| Document Reference   | FAB-MRM-FW-2024-v3.1                   |\\\\n|----------------------|----------------------------------------|\\\\n| Version              | 3.1                                    |\\\\n| Effective Date       | 1 January 2024                         |\\\\n| Owner                | Chief Risk Officer                     |\\\\n| Author               | Model Risk Management team, Group Risk |\\\\n| Approved By          | Board Risk & Compliance Committee      |\\\\n| Classification       | Confidential -Internal Use Only        |\\\\n\\\\n## 1. Framework Purpose and Scope\\\\n\\\\nThis Model Risk Management (MRM) Framework establishes FAB\\'s enterprise-wide standards for the identification, measurement, management, and governance of model risk. It applies to all quantitative models, AI/ML systems, and automated decision-making tools used within FAB Group that are classified as material under Section 2 of this Framework.\\\\n\\\\nThe Framework is aligned with internationally recognised standards, including the US Federal Reserve SR 11-7 guidance on model risk management and the CBUAE Circular No. 2024/BSE/047 on AI/ML governance.\\\\n\\\\nSCOPE: This Framework explicitly covers AI-based pricing recommendation systems, including large language model (LLM)-based agents used to propose credit facility pricing, as these systems generate quantitative outputs used in material business decisions.\\\\n\\\\n## 2. Model Risk Classification\\\\n\\\\nFAB classifies all models into three tiers based on materiality and the potential consequences of model error:\\\\n\\\\n| Tier            | Criteria                                                                                                   | Examples                                                             | Validation Requirement                                |\\\\n|-----------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Tier 1 (High)   | Direct P&L impact or credit decision influence above AED 10M; regulatory capital models; pricing AI agents | IFRS 9 impairment models · Credit pricing AI · Stress testing models | Full independent validation before deployment; annual |\\\\n| Tier 2 (Medium) | Indirect P&L impact; operational models with significant throughput                                        | Customer segmentation · behavioural scoring models                   | Targeted validation; biennial                         |\\\\n| Tier 3 (Low)    | Minimal impact; administrative or back-office tools                                                        | Document classification · internal scheduling tools                  | Self-certification by owner                           |\\\\n\\\\n## 3. Model Evidence Pack Requirements\\\\n\\\\nFor every Tier 1 and Tier 2 model deployed in production, a complete Model Evidence Pack (MEP) must be maintained and updated following any material model change. The MEP is the primary audit document for model risk purposes and must be made available to internal audit, CBUAE examiners, and external auditors upon request.\\\\n\\\\n## 3.1 Mandatory MEP Components - All Models\\\\n\\\\n1. Model Overview: Name, version, unique model ID, purpose, business function, date of initial development, and current deployment status.\\\\n2. Model Design Documentation: Theoretical basis, mathematical formulation, assumptions and limitations, data sources, and feature engineering approach.\\\\n3. Development and Training Data: Data dictionary, data sources, data quality assessment, training period, and any data preprocessing or transformations applied.\\\\n\\\\n4. Performance Metrics: In-sample and out-of-sample performance; benchmark comparison against challenger models or expert judgment; results of cross-validation.\\\\n5. Validation Record: Independent validation findings, identified weaknesses, management responses, and outstanding remediation actions.\\\\n6. Model Limitations and Known Failure Modes: Documented scenarios in which the model may perform poorly or produce unreliable outputs, and the controls in place to mitigate such failures.\\\\n7. Human Override Record: Summary statistics of human override rates for the preceding 12-month period, including trend analysis.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"source\": \"CBUAE_Circular_2024_BSE_047_AI_Governance\",\\n      \"clause\": \"3.2\",\\n      \"section\": \"3.2 Independent Model Validation\",\\n      \"effective_date\": \"\",\\n      \"stale\": false,\\n      \"score\": 0.0315,\\n      \"text\": \"## 3.2 Independent Model Validation\\\\n\\\\n3.2.1 All High-Risk AI/ML models (as classified under Article 4) must be independently validated before initial deployment and after any material modification. Independent validation means validation conducted by a team or function that is organisationally separate from the model development team. 3.2.2 The Model Validation Report must document: (a) a replication of the model\\'s development methodology; (b) performance benchmarking against alternative approaches; (c) sensitivity analysis; (d) out-of-sample performance testing; and (e) assessment of conceptual soundness of the model\\'s design. 3.2.3 For AI/ML models used in credit pricing, the Model Validation Report must additionally include: (a) a review of the pricing floor and floor compliance mechanism; (b) testing of the human oversight control (see\\\\n\\\\nArticle 5); and (c) assessment of the model\\'s behaviour under stressed market conditions.\\\\n\\\\n## Article 4 - AI Risk Classification\\\\n\\\\nLFIs must classify each AI/ML system in their inventory into one of three risk categories, based on the criteria in Table 4.1.\\\\n\\\\n| Risk Class   | Criteria                                                                                                                 | Examples                                                                   | Validation Required                                |\\\\n|--------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| HIGH         | Direct impact on credit decisions, pricing, lending limits, or exclusion/inclusion of customers from financial services. | Credit pricing agents · loan approval models · fraud decisioning systems   | Mandatory before deployment; annual re-validation  |\\\\n| MEDIUM       | Indirect influence on business decisions; used for internal analytics or monitoring with human review of outputs.        | Customer segmentation models · churn prediction · internal risk dashboards | Required within 6 months of deployment; biennial   |\\\\n| LOW          | No direct or indirect decision impact; used for operational efficiency or back-office automation.                        | Document classification · OCR · internal helpdesk chatbots                 | Not mandated; recommended at management discretion |\\\\n\\\\nCLASSIFICATION GUIDANCE: AI systems used to generate pricing recommendations for corporate credit facilities are HIGH risk under Article 4. This includes large language model (LLM)-based agents that produce pricing proposals, scenario analyses, or rate recommendations, even where human approval is required before the recommendation is acted upon.\\\\n\\\\n## Article 5 - Human Oversight and Control Requirements\\\\n\\\\n## 5.1 Mandatory Human-in-the-Loop Controls\\\\n\\\\n5.1.1 For all HIGH-risk AI/ML systems, LFIs must implement mandatory human review and approval controls that ensure no output from the AI/ML system is acted upon without explicit human authorisation by a qualified individual at the appropriate authority level. 5.1.2 The human reviewer must have access to: (a) the AI system\\'s recommendation in full; (b) the underlying data inputs used by the system; (c) the reasoning or explanation provided by the system for its recommendation; and (d) the relevant policy and regulatory context against which the recommendation should be assessed. 5.1.3 LFIs must implement technical controls to prevent the bypassing of human review steps. Evidence that the human review was completed, including the identity of the reviewer, the timestamp of review, and the reviewer\\'s decision, must be logged and retained for a minimum of seven years. 5.1.4 Where an AI system is used to generate pricing recommendations for credit facilities, the human reviewer\\\\n\\\\nmust confirm, in writing or through a system-logged approval, that: (a) the recommended pricing complies with all applicable internal policies; (b) the recommended pricing is not below the regulatory minimum standards; and (c) the reviewer has independently assessed and concurs with the recommendation.\\\\n\\\\n## 5.2 Override and Escalation\\\\n\\\\n5.2.1 LFIs must maintain documented override procedures that allow qualified personnel to deviate from an AI system\\'s recommendation. All overrides must be documented with the reason for override. 5.2.2 Systematic patterns of override (defined as override rates exceeding 25% of recommendations over a rolling 90-day period) must be reported to the AI Risk Officer and reviewed as a potential indicator of model\\\\n\\\\nperformance degradation.\\\\n\\\\n## Article 6 - Explainability and Transparency\\\\n\\\\n## 6.1 Explainability Requirements\\\\n\\\\n6.1.1 For every recommendation generated by a HIGH-risk AI system, the system must be capable of producing an explanation of the recommendation in plain language. This explanation must be made available to the human reviewer as part of the approval workflow.\\\\n\\\\n6.1.2 For credit pricing AI systems specifically, the explanation must include: (a) the key input signals that drove the recommendation (e.g., FTP rate, RWA, client risk rating, peer benchmarks); (b) a comparison of the recommendation against the applicable pricing floor; (c) an indication of the confidence level of the recommendation; and (d) a summary of any assumptions or data inputs that the system considers uncertain or incomplete.\\\\n\\\\n6.1.3 LFIs must retain records of AI-generated explanations alongside the corresponding recommendations for the same minimum retention period as the underlying credit documentation.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"source\": \"CBUAE_Circular_2024_BSE_047_AI_Governance\",\\n      \"clause\": \"2.1.1\",\\n      \"section\": \"CENTRAL BANK OF THE UNITED ARAB EMIRATES\",\\n      \"effective_date\": \"\",\\n      \"stale\": false,\\n      \"score\": 0.0308,\\n      \"text\": \"## CENTRAL BANK OF THE UNITED ARAB EMIRATES\\\\n\\\\nBanking Supervision &amp; Examination Department\\\\n\\\\n## SUPERVISORY CIRCULAR\\\\n\\\\n## No. 2024/BSE/047\\\\n\\\\n## Governance and Risk Management Requirements for Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning Systems in Licensed Financial Institutions\\\\n\\\\n| Circular Reference   | CBUAE/BSE/2024/047                                                                                 |\\\\n|----------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Issue Date           | 15 March 2024                                                                                      |\\\\n| Effective Date       | 1 July 2024                                                                                        |\\\\n| Addressed To         | Chief Executive Officers and Chief Risk Officers of all Licensed Financial Institutions in the UAE |\\\\n| Subject              | AI/ML Governance, Model Risk Management, and Consumer Protection Requirements                      |\\\\n| Supersedes           | CBUAE/BSE/2022/031 (Guidance on Automated Decision-Making)                                         |\\\\n| Compliance Deadline  | 31 December 2024 for Tier 1 institutions; 30 June 2025 for all others                              |\\\\n\\\\n## Article 1 - Purpose, Scope and Legal Basis\\\\n\\\\n1.1 This Circular is issued pursuant to the powers vested in the Central Bank of the UAE under Federal DecreeLaw No. 14 of 2018 regarding the Central Bank and Organisation of Financial Institutions and Activities, as amended, and the accompanying Executive Regulations. 1.2 This Circular establishes minimum standards for the governance, development, deployment, monitoring, and decommissioning of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) systems used by Licensed Financial Institutions (LFIs) in the UAE in connection with credit decisions, risk management, pricing of financial products, customer service, fraud detection, and any other material business function. 1.3 This Circular applies to all LFIs licensed and supervised by the Central Bank of the UAE, including but not limited to national banks, foreign bank branches, finance companies, exchange houses, and payment service providers, to the extent that they deploy AI or ML systems in material business processes. 1.4 For the purposes of this Circular, an AI/ML system is considered to be deployed in a \\'material business process\\' where its outputs directly or indirectly influence decisions that: (a) affect a customer\\'s access to or terms of a financial product or service; (b) generate recommendations that are presented to employees or customers as a basis for financial action; or (c) are used to calculate, estimate, or propose prices, rates, fees, or other commercial terms for financial products.\\\\n\\\\nIMPORTANT: The pricing of credit facilities, including the use of AI-generated pricing recommendations for corporate loans, revolving facilities, or any structured credit product, is explicitly classified as a material business process under Article 1.4(c). All requirements in this Circular apply to such systems.\\\\n\\\\n## Article 2 - Governance Framework Requirements\\\\n\\\\n## 2.1 Board and Senior Management Accountability\\\\n\\\\n2.1.1 The Board of Directors of every LFI deploying AI/ML systems in material business processes must adopt and maintain a written AI Governance Policy. This policy must be reviewed and re-approved by the Board at least annually. 2.1.2 A designated member of senior management, at a level no lower than Chief Risk Officer or equivalent, must be appointed as the AI Risk Officer responsible for overseeing the implementation of this Circular. 2.1.3 The AI Governance Policy must address, at minimum: (a) the LFI\\'s risk appetite for AI-related risks; (b) accountability structures for AI development and deployment; (c) human oversight requirements; (d) model\\\\n\\\\ninventory management; (e) consumer protection obligations; and (f) regulatory reporting obligations.\\\\n\\\\n## 2.2 AI Model Inventory\\\\n\\\\n2.2.1 Every LFI must maintain a centralised, up-to-date AI Model Inventory covering all AI/ML systems deployed in material business processes. The inventory must be made available to the Central Bank upon request.\\\\n\\\\n2.2.2 For each model in the inventory, the following minimum information must be recorded:\\\\n\\\\n- Model name, version, and unique identifier.\\\\n- Business function and processes in which the model is deployed.\\\\n- Date of initial deployment and date of most recent material update.\\\\n- Model owner (business function) and model developer (technology/data team).\\\\n- Validation status and date of last independent validation.\\\\n- Current monitoring status (active / under review / decommissioned).\\\\n- Risk classification (High / Medium / Low) as determined under Article 4.\\\\n\\\\n## Article 3 - Model Risk Management\\\\n\\\\n## 3.1 Model Development Standards\\\\n\\\\n3.1.1 All AI/ML models deployed in material business processes must be developed in accordance with sound model development practices, including but not limited to: clearly documented model objectives; appropriate training data governance; out-of-time and out-of-sample testing; and documentation of model limitations and known failure modes.\\\\n\\\\n3.1.2 Training data used for models that influence credit pricing or credit decisions must be sourced from reliable, auditable data systems. Data lineage must be documented from source to model input.\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \"total_results\": 3,\\n  \"freshness_warning\": false,\\n  \"latency_ms\": 326.06\\n}'}]}"}
{"ts": "2026-06-26T11:38:55+0530", "level": "INFO", "logger": "agent_framework", "trace_id": "6171ac96850ea8387bcf401d58a12012", "span_id": "1e7de0b98d7a52d0", "parent_span_id": "-", "request_id": "981D9CEA", "msg": "{'role': 'assistant', 'parts': [{'type': 'tool_call', 'id': 'z2f94re3n', 'name': 'search_documents', 'arguments': '{\"query\":\"senior management involvement model approval oversight escalation FAB Model Risk Management Framework\",\"top_k\":5}'}]}"}
{"ts": "2026-06-26T11:38:55+0530", "level": "INFO", "logger": "agent_framework", "trace_id": "6171ac96850ea8387bcf401d58a12012", "span_id": "1e7de0b98d7a52d0", "parent_span_id": "-", "request_id": "981D9CEA", "msg": "{'role': 'tool', 'parts': [{'type': 'tool_call_response', 'id': 'z2f94re3n', 'response': '{\\n  \"results\": [\\n    {\\n      \"source\": \"FAB_Model_Risk_Management_Framework_v3.1\",\\n      \"clause\": \"Section 2\",\\n      \"section\": \"FIRST ABU DHABI BANK\",\\n      \"effective_date\": \"\",\\n      \"stale\": false,\\n      \"score\": 0.0328,\\n      \"text\": \"## FIRST ABU DHABI BANK\\\\n\\\\nGroup Risk Management\\\\n\\\\n## MODEL RISK MANAGEMENT FRAMEWORK\\\\n\\\\nAI / ML and Quantitative Model Governance Standards\\\\n\\\\n| Document Reference   | FAB-MRM-FW-2024-v3.1                   |\\\\n|----------------------|----------------------------------------|\\\\n| Version              | 3.1                                    |\\\\n| Effective Date       | 1 January 2024                         |\\\\n| Owner                | Chief Risk Officer                     |\\\\n| Author               | Model Risk Management team, Group Risk |\\\\n| Approved By          | Board Risk & Compliance Committee      |\\\\n| Classification       | Confidential -Internal Use Only        |\\\\n\\\\n## 1. Framework Purpose and Scope\\\\n\\\\nThis Model Risk Management (MRM) Framework establishes FAB\\'s enterprise-wide standards for the identification, measurement, management, and governance of model risk. It applies to all quantitative models, AI/ML systems, and automated decision-making tools used within FAB Group that are classified as material under Section 2 of this Framework.\\\\n\\\\nThe Framework is aligned with internationally recognised standards, including the US Federal Reserve SR 11-7 guidance on model risk management and the CBUAE Circular No. 2024/BSE/047 on AI/ML governance.\\\\n\\\\nSCOPE: This Framework explicitly covers AI-based pricing recommendation systems, including large language model (LLM)-based agents used to propose credit facility pricing, as these systems generate quantitative outputs used in material business decisions.\\\\n\\\\n## 2. Model Risk Classification\\\\n\\\\nFAB classifies all models into three tiers based on materiality and the potential consequences of model error:\\\\n\\\\n| Tier            | Criteria                                                                                                   | Examples                                                             | Validation Requirement                                |\\\\n|-----------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Tier 1 (High)   | Direct P&L impact or credit decision influence above AED 10M; regulatory capital models; pricing AI agents | IFRS 9 impairment models · Credit pricing AI · Stress testing models | Full independent validation before deployment; annual |\\\\n| Tier 2 (Medium) | Indirect P&L impact; operational models with significant throughput                                        | Customer segmentation · behavioural scoring models                   | Targeted validation; biennial                         |\\\\n| Tier 3 (Low)    | Minimal impact; administrative or back-office tools                                                        | Document classification · internal scheduling tools                  | Self-certification by owner                           |\\\\n\\\\n## 3. Model Evidence Pack Requirements\\\\n\\\\nFor every Tier 1 and Tier 2 model deployed in production, a complete Model Evidence Pack (MEP) must be maintained and updated following any material model change. The MEP is the primary audit document for model risk purposes and must be made available to internal audit, CBUAE examiners, and external auditors upon request.\\\\n\\\\n## 3.1 Mandatory MEP Components - All Models\\\\n\\\\n1. Model Overview: Name, version, unique model ID, purpose, business function, date of initial development, and current deployment status.\\\\n2. Model Design Documentation: Theoretical basis, mathematical formulation, assumptions and limitations, data sources, and feature engineering approach.\\\\n3. Development and Training Data: Data dictionary, data sources, data quality assessment, training period, and any data preprocessing or transformations applied.\\\\n\\\\n4. Performance Metrics: In-sample and out-of-sample performance; benchmark comparison against challenger models or expert judgment; results of cross-validation.\\\\n5. Validation Record: Independent validation findings, identified weaknesses, management responses, and outstanding remediation actions.\\\\n6. Model Limitations and Known Failure Modes: Documented scenarios in which the model may perform poorly or produce unreliable outputs, and the controls in place to mitigate such failures.\\\\n7. Human Override Record: Summary statistics of human override rates for the preceding 12-month period, including trend analysis.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"source\": \"CBUAE_Circular_2024_BSE_047_AI_Governance\",\\n      \"clause\": \"2.1.1\",\\n      \"section\": \"CENTRAL BANK OF THE UNITED ARAB EMIRATES\",\\n      \"effective_date\": \"\",\\n      \"stale\": false,\\n      \"score\": 0.0317,\\n      \"text\": \"## CENTRAL BANK OF THE UNITED ARAB EMIRATES\\\\n\\\\nBanking Supervision &amp; Examination Department\\\\n\\\\n## SUPERVISORY CIRCULAR\\\\n\\\\n## No. 2024/BSE/047\\\\n\\\\n## Governance and Risk Management Requirements for Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning Systems in Licensed Financial Institutions\\\\n\\\\n| Circular Reference   | CBUAE/BSE/2024/047                                                                                 |\\\\n|----------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Issue Date           | 15 March 2024                                                                                      |\\\\n| Effective Date       | 1 July 2024                                                                                        |\\\\n| Addressed To         | Chief Executive Officers and Chief Risk Officers of all Licensed Financial Institutions in the UAE |\\\\n| Subject              | AI/ML Governance, Model Risk Management, and Consumer Protection Requirements                      |\\\\n| Supersedes           | CBUAE/BSE/2022/031 (Guidance on Automated Decision-Making)                                         |\\\\n| Compliance Deadline  | 31 December 2024 for Tier 1 institutions; 30 June 2025 for all others                              |\\\\n\\\\n## Article 1 - Purpose, Scope and Legal Basis\\\\n\\\\n1.1 This Circular is issued pursuant to the powers vested in the Central Bank of the UAE under Federal DecreeLaw No. 14 of 2018 regarding the Central Bank and Organisation of Financial Institutions and Activities, as amended, and the accompanying Executive Regulations. 1.2 This Circular establishes minimum standards for the governance, development, deployment, monitoring, and decommissioning of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) systems used by Licensed Financial Institutions (LFIs) in the UAE in connection with credit decisions, risk management, pricing of financial products, customer service, fraud detection, and any other material business function. 1.3 This Circular applies to all LFIs licensed and supervised by the Central Bank of the UAE, including but not limited to national banks, foreign bank branches, finance companies, exchange houses, and payment service providers, to the extent that they deploy AI or ML systems in material business processes. 1.4 For the purposes of this Circular, an AI/ML system is considered to be deployed in a \\'material business process\\' where its outputs directly or indirectly influence decisions that: (a) affect a customer\\'s access to or terms of a financial product or service; (b) generate recommendations that are presented to employees or customers as a basis for financial action; or (c) are used to calculate, estimate, or propose prices, rates, fees, or other commercial terms for financial products.\\\\n\\\\nIMPORTANT: The pricing of credit facilities, including the use of AI-generated pricing recommendations for corporate loans, revolving facilities, or any structured credit product, is explicitly classified as a material business process under Article 1.4(c). All requirements in this Circular apply to such systems.\\\\n\\\\n## Article 2 - Governance Framework Requirements\\\\n\\\\n## 2.1 Board and Senior Management Accountability\\\\n\\\\n2.1.1 The Board of Directors of every LFI deploying AI/ML systems in material business processes must adopt and maintain a written AI Governance Policy. This policy must be reviewed and re-approved by the Board at least annually. 2.1.2 A designated member of senior management, at a level no lower than Chief Risk Officer or equivalent, must be appointed as the AI Risk Officer responsible for overseeing the implementation of this Circular. 2.1.3 The AI Governance Policy must address, at minimum: (a) the LFI\\'s risk appetite for AI-related risks; (b) accountability structures for AI development and deployment; (c) human oversight requirements; (d) model\\\\n\\\\ninventory management; (e) consumer protection obligations; and (f) regulatory reporting obligations.\\\\n\\\\n## 2.2 AI Model Inventory\\\\n\\\\n2.2.1 Every LFI must maintain a centralised, up-to-date AI Model Inventory covering all AI/ML systems deployed in material business processes. The inventory must be made available to the Central Bank upon request.\\\\n\\\\n2.2.2 For each model in the inventory, the following minimum information must be recorded:\\\\n\\\\n- Model name, version, and unique identifier.\\\\n- Business function and processes in which the model is deployed.\\\\n- Date of initial deployment and date of most recent material update.\\\\n- Model owner (business function) and model developer (technology/data team).\\\\n- Validation status and date of last independent validation.\\\\n- Current monitoring status (active / under review / decommissioned).\\\\n- Risk classification (High / Medium / Low) as determined under Article 4.\\\\n\\\\n## Article 3 - Model Risk Management\\\\n\\\\n## 3.1 Model Development Standards\\\\n\\\\n3.1.1 All AI/ML models deployed in material business processes must be developed in accordance with sound model development practices, including but not limited to: clearly documented model objectives; appropriate training data governance; out-of-time and out-of-sample testing; and documentation of model limitations and known failure modes.\\\\n\\\\n3.1.2 Training data used for models that influence credit pricing or credit decisions must be sourced from reliable, auditable data systems. Data lineage must be documented from source to model input.\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \"total_results\": 2,\\n  \"freshness_warning\": false,\\n  \"latency_ms\": 280.16\\n}'}]}"}
