{"ts": "2026-07-03T13:49:34+0530", "level": "INFO", "logger": "agent_framework", "trace_id": "66f49fa6d04025f90916ef3b3abf70f7", "span_id": "3df4b6a87688c614", "parent_span_id": "-", "request_id": "9DE1C0B2", "msg": "{'role': 'tool', 'parts': [{'type': 'tool_call_response', 'id': 'q9hje40vy', 'response': '{\\n  \"results\": [\\n    {\\n      \"source\": \"FAB_Corporate_Term_Loan_Product_Manual_v2.0\",\\n      \"clause\": \"3.1\",\\n      \"section\": \"FIRST ABU DHABI BANK\",\\n      \"effective_date\": \"\",\\n      \"stale\": false,\\n      \"score\": 0.0333,\\n      \"text\": \"## FIRST ABU DHABI BANK\\\\n\\\\nCorporate Banking - Product Management\\\\n\\\\n## CORPORATE TERM LOAN - PRODUCT MANUAL\\\\n\\\\nProduct Structure, Eligibility, Pricing, and Term Sheet Standards\\\\n\\\\n| Document Reference   | FAB-PM-CTL-2024-v2.0                                    |\\\\n|----------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Product              | Corporate Term Loan (AED and Foreign Currency)          |\\\\n| Effective Date       | 1 March 2024                                            |\\\\n| Owner                | Head of Corporate Lending Products                      |\\\\n| Target Segment       | Corporate clients with annual turnover > AED 50 million |\\\\n| Classification       | Internal -Relationship Manager Use Only                 |\\\\n\\\\n## 1. Product Overview\\\\n\\\\nFAB\\'s Corporate Term Loan (CTL) is a bilateral lending product designed for corporate clients seeking mediumto-long-term financing for capital expenditure, project finance, acquisition finance, or general corporate purposes. The CTL is FAB\\'s primary relationship lending product in the corporate segment and is the product type most frequently processed through the GERNAS Pricing Agent.\\\\n\\\\n## 1.1 Product Parameters\\\\n\\\\n| Parameter             | Specification                                                                                            |\\\\n|-----------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Minimum Facility Size | AED 5,000,000 (or foreign currency equivalent)                                                           |\\\\n| Maximum Facility Size | Subject to single-obligor limits (FAB-CRP-CONC-2024-v1.8)                                                |\\\\n| Eligible Tenors       | 12, 24, 36, 48, 60, 72, 84 months (standard); longer tenors require GCC approval                         |\\\\n| Eligible Currencies   | AED, USD, EUR, GBP, JPY, CNY (CNY subject to treasury availability)                                      |\\\\n| Interest Rate Type    | Floating (EIBOR/SOFR + spread) or fixed rate                                                             |\\\\n| Repayment Structure   | Bullet, amortising (equal instalments), or sculpted (cash flow linked)                                   |\\\\n| Security              | Unsecured (investment grade) or secured (see Section 3)                                                  |\\\\n| Eligible Purpose      | Capex, acquisition, working capital supplement, refinancing (not margin lending or speculative purposes) |\\\\n\\\\n## 2. Eligibility Criteria\\\\n\\\\n## 2.1 Client Eligibility\\\\n\\\\nThe Corporate Term Loan is available to clients that meet all of the following eligibility criteria:\\\\n\\\\n- Incorporated and operating entity with audited financial statements for a minimum of two years.\\\\n- Annual turnover exceeding AED 50 million (or foreign currency equivalent) as evidenced by audited accounts.\\\\n- FAB internal credit rating of B or above (CCC and below are not eligible without GCC waiver).\\\\n- Clean KYC status - no outstanding AML flags or sanctions screening alerts.\\\\n- Satisfactory credit bureau check with no material adverse information within the preceding 24 months.\\\\n\\\\n## 2.2 Excluded Purposes\\\\n\\\\nThe Corporate Term Loan may not be used for the following purposes:\\\\n\\\\n- Margin lending, trading in securities, or investment in mutual funds or hedge funds.\\\\n- Financing of activities prohibited under UAE law or FAB\\'s Prohibited Sectors List (PSL-2024).\\\\n- Refinancing of existing FAB facilities at below-market pricing without proper exception approval.\\\\n\\\\n## 3. Pricing Structure\\\\n\\\\n## 3.1 Standard Pricing Components\\\\n\\\\nThe all-in pricing for a Corporate Term Loan comprises the following components, all of which must be included in the pricing model submitted for credit approval:\\\\n\\\\n| Pricing Component      | Typical Range                 | Description                                                                                                      |\\\\n|------------------------|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Base Rate (FTP)        | Set by Treasury monthly       | Matched-maturity internal cost of funds; AED EIBOR- based for AED facilities; SOFR-based for USD                 |\\\\n| Credit Spread (Margin) | 65-450 bps over FTP           | Risk-based spread reflecting client rating, security, and tenor; subject to floors in FAB-POL-PRICE-2024- v2.4   |\\\\n| Arrangement Fee        | 30-100 bps of facility amount | One-time fee payable at signing or first drawdown; included in RAROC calculation on annualised basis             |\\\\n| Commitment Fee         | 0-40 bps p.a. on undrawn      | Applicable to facilities with availability period > 30 days; standard commitment fee is 35% of applicable margin |\\\\n| Agency Fee             | USD 15,000 - 50,000 p.a.      | Applicable where FAB acts as Facility Agent in syndicated structures                                             |\\\\n\\\\n## 3.2 Pricing Floors - Reference to Credit Pricing Policy\\\\n\\\\nMinimum pricing floors applicable to all Corporate Term Loans are governed by FAB\\'s Credit Pricing Policy (FABPOL-PRICE-2024-v2.4), Section 3.1. The GERNAS Pricing Agent will automatically retrieve applicable floors from the Knowledge Graph based on the client\\'s credit rating, facility tenor, and currency. Relationship Managers must not override the floor without a documented Pricing Exception as per Section 6 of the Credit Pricing Policy.\\\\n\\\\nPRICING AGENT USERS: When using the GERNAS Pricing Agent for CTL pricing, input the following parameters to ensure correct floor retrieval: product\\\\\\\\_type = \\'corporate\\\\\\\\_term\\\\\\\\_loan\\', tenor\\\\\\\\_months = [actual months], currency = [AED/USD/etc], client\\\\\\\\_rating = [FAB internal rating]. The agent will retrieve the applicable floor from the Knowledge Graph and validate all scenarios against it.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"source\": \"FAB_Credit_Pricing_Policy_v2.4\",\\n      \"clause\": \"Section 6\",\\n      \"section\": \"FIRST ABU DHABI BANK\",\\n      \"effective_date\": \"\",\\n      \"stale\": false,\\n      \"score\": 0.0325,\\n      \"text\": \"## FIRST ABU DHABI BANK\\\\n\\\\nCommercial Banking - Credit &amp; Pricing Division\\\\n\\\\n## CREDIT PRICING POLICY\\\\n\\\\nCorporate &amp; Commercial Banking - Lending Products\\\\n\\\\n| Document Reference   | FAB-POL-PRICE-2024-v2.4                     |\\\\n|----------------------|---------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Effective Date       | 1 June 2024                                 |\\\\n| Review Date          | 31 May 2025                                 |\\\\n| Approved By          | Chief Commercial Officer, FAB               |\\\\n| Owner                | Head of Credit Pricing, Commercial Banking  |\\\\n| Classification       | STRICTLY CONFIDENTIAL -Internal Use Only    |\\\\n| Replaces             | FAB-POL-PRICE-2023-v2.3 dated 15 March 2023 |\\\\n\\\\n## 1. Purpose and Scope\\\\n\\\\nThis policy establishes the minimum pricing standards, approval authority requirements, and governance controls applicable to all credit facilities extended by First Abu Dhabi Bank (FAB) under its Corporate and Commercial Banking franchise. It applies to all relationship managers, credit officers, and product teams involved in the origination, structuring, or repricing of credit facilities.\\\\n\\\\nThis policy is designed to ensure that all credit pricing decisions:\\\\n\\\\n- Recover FAB\\'s fully loaded cost of funds, capital, and operational costs.\\\\n- Generate risk-adjusted returns commensurate with the credit risk assumed.\\\\n- Comply with applicable UAE Central Bank (CBUAE) regulations and FAB\\'s own risk appetite.\\\\n- Are documented, approved at the appropriate authority level, and auditable.\\\\n\\\\nSCOPE: This policy applies to all corporate term loans, revolving credit facilities, syndicated loans, trade finance facilities, and treasury credit products originated through FAB\\'s Corporate and Commercial Banking division. Retail lending is governed by a separate policy (FAB-POL-PRICE-RETAIL-2024).\\\\n\\\\n## 1.1 Definitions\\\\n\\\\n| Term                       | Definition                                                                                                                                                            |\\\\n|----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Floor Rate                 | The minimum all-in pricing rate for a credit facility, below which no transaction may be executed without Pricing Exception approval.                                 |\\\\n| All-In Rate                | The total annualised yield to FAB from a facility, including margin, upfront fees (annualised), commitment fees, and any other recurring fee income.                  |\\\\n| FTP (Funds Transfer Price) | The internal cost of funds allocated to each facility based on tenor, currency, and product type, as determined by FAB\\'s Treasury function on a monthly basis.        |\\\\n| RWA(Risk-Weighted Assets)  | The regulatory capital measure calculated by applying Basel III risk weights to the facility exposure, taking into account the counterparty\\'s internal credit rating. |\\\\n| Capital Cost               | The annual cost of regulatory capital consumed by a facility, calculated as RWA × FAB\\'s internal cost of equity hurdle rate.                                          |\\\\n| NIM (Net Interest Margin)  | The difference between the all-in facility rate and the applicable FTP rate, representing the gross spread earned by the business unit.                               |\\\\n| RAROC                      | Risk-Adjusted Return on Capital -the primary profitability metric used by FAB to evaluate credit transactions.                                                        |\\\\n| Relationship Value         | An adjustment to pricing that recognises the full revenue contribution of a client relationship across all products and services with FAB.                            |\\\\n\\\\n## 2. Pricing Principles\\\\n\\\\nAll credit pricing at FAB is based on four fundamental principles that must be satisfied simultaneously for a transaction to be approved:\\\\n\\\\n## 2.1 Cost Recovery\\\\n\\\\nEvery credit facility must, at a minimum, recover FAB\\'s fully loaded cost of funds (FTP), allocated capital cost, operating cost allocation, and expected credit loss provision. A transaction priced below full cost recovery may only be approved under Section 6 (Pricing Exceptions) of this policy.\\\\n\\\\nThe minimum cost recovery calculation is:\\\\n\\\\n```\\\\nMinimum Rate = FTP Rate + Capital Cost (bps) + Operating Cost Allocation (bps) + Expected Credit Loss Provision (bps)\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n## 2.2 Risk-Adjusted Profitability\\\\n\\\\nBeyond cost recovery, credit facilities must generate a RAROC that meets or exceeds FAB\\'s minimum acceptable return on equity (RoE) hurdle, as set by Group Finance and reviewed annually. The current RoE hurdle is 13.5% post-tax.\\\\n\\\\n## RAROC is calculated as:\\\\n\\\\nRAROC = (All-In Revenue - Expected Losses - Operating Costs) / Regulatory Capital\\\\n\\\\nIMPORTANT: Transactions with RAROC below the 13.5% hurdle may still be approved if they meet the Relationship Value criteria in Section 2.4 below, subject to appropriate approval authority.\\\\n\\\\n## 2.3 Market Competitiveness\\\\n\\\\nPricing must reflect prevailing market conditions for comparable transactions. Relationship Managers are required to provide market evidence (peer bank indicative pricing, publicly available benchmarks, or Bloomberg comparable data) for all transactions above AED 25 million. The Credit Pricing Committee maintains a quarterly market pricing survey that serves as the primary benchmark for this requirement.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"source\": \"FAB_Model_Risk_Management_Framework_v3.1\",\\n      \"clause\": \"3.2\",\\n      \"section\": \"3.2 Additional MEP Components - AI/ML Pricing Models\",\\n      \"effective_date\": \"\",\\n      \"stale\": false,\\n      \"score\": 0.0317,\\n      \"text\": \"## 3.2 Additional MEP Components - AI/ML Pricing Models\\\\n\\\\nFor AI/ML systems used in credit pricing (Tier 1), the MEP must additionally include:\\\\n\\\\n8. LLM and Agent Architecture: Description of the LLM backbone, agent orchestration framework, toolcalling design, and memory architecture. Version control information for all model components.\\\\n9. Pricing Floor Compliance Mechanism: Technical description of how the model enforces pricing floor constraints. Evidence that the system is incapable of recommending a rate below the applicable floor without generating a policy exception flag.\\\\n10. Data Grounding Evidence: Documentation of the RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) architecture including: vector database configuration, document sources indexed, embedding model specifications, retrieval strategy, and re-indexing schedule.\\\\n11. Knowledge Graph Schema: For systems using a Knowledge Graph for rule lookup, documentation of the graph schema, node types, edge types, and data population procedures.\\\\n12. Explainability Artefact: A sample model output with the accompanying explanation, demonstrating that the explanation satisfies the requirements of CBUAE Circular 2024/BSE/047 Article 6.\\\\n13. Human-in-the-Loop Control Evidence: Technical description of the interrupt mechanism, approval workflow, and audit log structure. Confirmation that bypassing human approval is technically prevented.\\\\n14. Hallucination Risk Assessment: Assessment of the risk that the model may generate factually incorrect pricing information and the controls in place to detect and prevent such errors reaching end-users.\\\\n\\\\nIMPORTANT: The Data Grounding Evidence section (item 3 above) must demonstrate that all pricing floor values referenced by the model are sourced from the Knowledge Graph or RAG system, and not from the LLM\\'s parametric knowledge. Parametric knowledge is not auditable and may not reflect current policy.\\\\n\\\\n## 4. Ongoing Monitoring Requirements\\\\n\\\\n## 4.1 Performance Monitoring\\\\n\\\\nAll production AI/ML pricing models must be monitored monthly against the following metrics:\\\\n\\\\n| Metric                         | Monitoring Frequency   | Alert Threshold                                   |\\\\n|--------------------------------|------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Pricing floor breach rate      | Daily                  | Any breach triggers immediate investigation       |\\\\n| Human override rate            | Monthly                | > 20% override rate triggers model review         |\\\\n| Recommendation acceptance rate | Monthly                | < 60% acceptance rate triggers performance review |\\\\n\\\\n| RAG retrieval freshness score   | Weekly   | Average freshness < 0.80 triggers re- indexing             |\\\\n|---------------------------------|----------|------------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Knowledge Graph staleness       | Weekly   | Any node with effective_date > 6 months old without review |\\\\n| LLM response latency            | Daily    | p95 > 5 seconds triggers infrastructure review             |\\\\n| Explanation completeness score  | Monthly  | < 90% complete explanations triggers model review          |\\\\n\\\\n## 5. Incident Reporting\\\\n\\\\nA model incident is defined as any event in which an AI/ML model produces an output that: (a) breaches a pricing floor; (b) results in customer detriment; (c) triggers a regulatory notification; or (d) results in a financial loss attributable to model error.\\\\n\\\\nAll model incidents must be reported to the Model Risk Management team within 24 hours of detection, and to the CBUAE within 30 calendar days where the incident is material (threshold: financial impact &gt; AED 1 million or regulatory breach).\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"source\": \"CBUAE_Circular_2024_BSE_047_AI_Governance\",\\n      \"clause\": \"Article 1.4\",\\n      \"section\": \"CENTRAL BANK OF THE UNITED ARAB EMIRATES\",\\n      \"effective_date\": \"\",\\n      \"stale\": false,\\n      \"score\": 0.0315,\\n      \"text\": \"## CENTRAL BANK OF THE UNITED ARAB EMIRATES\\\\n\\\\nBanking Supervision &amp; Examination Department\\\\n\\\\n## SUPERVISORY CIRCULAR\\\\n\\\\n## No. 2024/BSE/047\\\\n\\\\n## Governance and Risk Management Requirements for Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning Systems in Licensed Financial Institutions\\\\n\\\\n| Circular Reference   | CBUAE/BSE/2024/047                                                                                 |\\\\n|----------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Issue Date           | 15 March 2024                                                                                      |\\\\n| Effective Date       | 1 July 2024                                                                                        |\\\\n| Addressed To         | Chief Executive Officers and Chief Risk Officers of all Licensed Financial Institutions in the UAE |\\\\n| Subject              | AI/ML Governance, Model Risk Management, and Consumer Protection Requirements                      |\\\\n| Supersedes           | CBUAE/BSE/2022/031 (Guidance on Automated Decision-Making)                                         |\\\\n| Compliance Deadline  | 31 December 2024 for Tier 1 institutions; 30 June 2025 for all others                              |\\\\n\\\\n## Article 1 - Purpose, Scope and Legal Basis\\\\n\\\\n1.1 This Circular is issued pursuant to the powers vested in the Central Bank of the UAE under Federal DecreeLaw No. 14 of 2018 regarding the Central Bank and Organisation of Financial Institutions and Activities, as amended, and the accompanying Executive Regulations. 1.2 This Circular establishes minimum standards for the governance, development, deployment, monitoring, and decommissioning of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) systems used by Licensed Financial Institutions (LFIs) in the UAE in connection with credit decisions, risk management, pricing of financial products, customer service, fraud detection, and any other material business function. 1.3 This Circular applies to all LFIs licensed and supervised by the Central Bank of the UAE, including but not limited to national banks, foreign bank branches, finance companies, exchange houses, and payment service providers, to the extent that they deploy AI or ML systems in material business processes. 1.4 For the purposes of this Circular, an AI/ML system is considered to be deployed in a \\'material business process\\' where its outputs directly or indirectly influence decisions that: (a) affect a customer\\'s access to or terms of a financial product or service; (b) generate recommendations that are presented to employees or customers as a basis for financial action; or (c) are used to calculate, estimate, or propose prices, rates, fees, or other commercial terms for financial products.\\\\n\\\\nIMPORTANT: The pricing of credit facilities, including the use of AI-generated pricing recommendations for corporate loans, revolving facilities, or any structured credit product, is explicitly classified as a material business process under Article 1.4(c). All requirements in this Circular apply to such systems.\\\\n\\\\n## Article 2 - Governance Framework Requirements\\\\n\\\\n## 2.1 Board and Senior Management Accountability\\\\n\\\\n2.1.1 The Board of Directors of every LFI deploying AI/ML systems in material business processes must adopt and maintain a written AI Governance Policy. This policy must be reviewed and re-approved by the Board at least annually. 2.1.2 A designated member of senior management, at a level no lower than Chief Risk Officer or equivalent, must be appointed as the AI Risk Officer responsible for overseeing the implementation of this Circular. 2.1.3 The AI Governance Policy must address, at minimum: (a) the LFI\\'s risk appetite for AI-related risks; (b) accountability structures for AI development and deployment; (c) human oversight requirements; (d) model\\\\n\\\\ninventory management; (e) consumer protection obligations; and (f) regulatory reporting obligations.\\\\n\\\\n## 2.2 AI Model Inventory\\\\n\\\\n2.2.1 Every LFI must maintain a centralised, up-to-date AI Model Inventory covering all AI/ML systems deployed in material business processes. The inventory must be made available to the Central Bank upon request.\\\\n\\\\n2.2.2 For each model in the inventory, the following minimum information must be recorded:\\\\n\\\\n- Model name, version, and unique identifier.\\\\n- Business function and processes in which the model is deployed.\\\\n- Date of initial deployment and date of most recent material update.\\\\n- Model owner (business function) and model developer (technology/data team).\\\\n- Validation status and date of last independent validation.\\\\n- Current monitoring status (active / under review / decommissioned).\\\\n- Risk classification (High / Medium / Low) as determined under Article 4.\\\\n\\\\n## Article 3 - Model Risk Management\\\\n\\\\n## 3.1 Model Development Standards\\\\n\\\\n3.1.1 All AI/ML models deployed in material business processes must be developed in accordance with sound model development practices, including but not limited to: clearly documented model objectives; appropriate training data governance; out-of-time and out-of-sample testing; and documentation of model limitations and known failure modes.\\\\n\\\\n3.1.2 Training data used for models that influence credit pricing or credit decisions must be sourced from reliable, auditable data systems. Data lineage must be documented from source to model input.\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \"total_results\": 4,\\n  \"freshness_warning\": false,\\n  \"latency_ms\": 183.2\\n}'}]}"}
{"ts": "2026-07-03T13:49:34+0530", "level": "INFO", "logger": "openai._base_client", "trace_id": "66f49fa6d04025f90916ef3b3abf70f7", "span_id": "3df4b6a87688c614", "parent_span_id": "-", "request_id": "9DE1C0B2", "msg": "Retrying request to /chat/completions in 5.000000 seconds"}
{"ts": "2026-07-03T13:49:44+0530", "level": "INFO", "logger": "agent_framework", "trace_id": "66f49fa6d04025f90916ef3b3abf70f7", "span_id": "3df4b6a87688c614", "parent_span_id": "-", "request_id": "9DE1C0B2", "msg": "{'role': 'assistant', 'parts': [{'type': 'text', 'content': 'Based on FAB\\'s current Credit Pricing Policy and Corporate Term Loan Product Manual, the loan pricing methodology is a risk-adjusted, cost-recovery model designed to ensure pricing meets the bank’s profitability hurdles while remaining competitive.\\n\\nHere are the standard components and calculation steps for determining a recommended loan price:\\n\\n### 1. Pricing Components ([Source: `FAB_Corporate_Term_Loan_Product_Manual_v2.0` · Section 3.1])\\nThe \"All-In\" recommended price consists of both the interest margin and annualized fee income. The standard components include:\\n\\n*   **Base Rate (FTP):** The internal Funds Transfer Price set by Treasury monthly based on matched-maturity cost of funds (e.g., EIBOR plus spread for AED; SOFR plus spread for USD).\\n*   **Credit Spread (Margin):** A risk-based premium over the FTP (typically ranging from 65 to 450 bps) reflecting the client\\'s internal credit rating, security, and tenor.\\n*   **Arrangement Fee:** A one-time upfront fee (typically 30–100 bps of the facility amount) which is annualized for profitability calculations.\\n*   **Commitment Fee:** An annualized fee on undrawn amounts (standard is 35% of the applicable margin).\\n*   **Agency Fee (Optional):** Applicable only if FAB acts as a Facility Agent in syndicated loans.\\n\\n### 2. Calculation Steps for Recommended Price ([Source: `FAB_Credit_Pricing_Policy_v2.4` · Sections 2.1, 2.2])\\n\\nThe pricing calculation follows a structured hierarchy to ensure cost coverage and return targets are met:\\n\\n**Step 1: Establish the Minimum Cost Recovery Rate**\\nBefore any profit margin is added, the price must cover FAB\\'s fully loaded costs. \\n*   **Formula:** `Minimum Rate = FTP Rate + Capital Cost + Operating Cost Allocation + Expected Credit Loss (ECL) Provision`\\n\\n**Step 2: Determine Risk-Adjusted Return on Capital (RAROC)**\\nThe primary profitability metric used to set the final recommendation is RAROC. The total price must generate a RAROC that meets or exceeds FAB’s minimum post-tax Return on Equity (RoE) hurdle (currently 13.5%).\\n*   **Formula:** `RAROC = (All-In Revenue - Expected Losses - Operating Costs) / Regulatory Capital`\\n\\n**Step 3: Formulate the All-In Rate**\\nThe final recommended price aggregates the margin, the annualized upfront arrangement fee, and the ongoing commitment fee to reach the RAROC target.\\n\\n**Step 4: Validate Against Pricing Floors & Market**\\n*   **Floor Check:** The recommended price is cross-referenced against hard pricing floors in the Knowledge Graph (based on rating, tenor, and currency). RMs cannot override these floors without a documented Pricing Exception. \\n*   **Market Check:** For transactions exceeding AED 25 million, the price must be aligned with peer bank indications or market benchmarks (as per `FAB Credit Pricing Policy` Section 2.3).'}]}"}
{"ts": "2026-07-03T13:49:44+0530", "level": "INFO", "logger": "agent_framework", "trace_id": "66f49fa6d04025f90916ef3b3abf70f7", "span_id": "a6e8a011107867a1", "parent_span_id": "-", "request_id": "9DE1C0B2", "msg": "{'role': 'assistant', 'parts': [{'type': 'tool_call', 'id': 'q9hje40vy', 'name': 'search_documents', 'arguments': '{\"query\":\"loan pricing methodology components calculation steps recommended price\",\"top_k\":5}'}]}"}
{"ts": "2026-07-03T13:49:44+0530", "level": "INFO", "logger": "agent_framework", "trace_id": "66f49fa6d04025f90916ef3b3abf70f7", "span_id": "a6e8a011107867a1", "parent_span_id": "-", "request_id": "9DE1C0B2", "msg": "{'role': 'tool', 'parts': [{'type': 'tool_call_response', 'id': 'q9hje40vy', 'response': '{\\n  \"results\": [\\n    {\\n      \"source\": \"FAB_Corporate_Term_Loan_Product_Manual_v2.0\",\\n      \"clause\": \"3.1\",\\n      \"section\": \"FIRST ABU DHABI BANK\",\\n      \"effective_date\": \"\",\\n      \"stale\": false,\\n      \"score\": 0.0333,\\n      \"text\": \"## FIRST ABU DHABI BANK\\\\n\\\\nCorporate Banking - Product Management\\\\n\\\\n## CORPORATE TERM LOAN - PRODUCT MANUAL\\\\n\\\\nProduct Structure, Eligibility, Pricing, and Term Sheet Standards\\\\n\\\\n| Document Reference   | FAB-PM-CTL-2024-v2.0                                    |\\\\n|----------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Product              | Corporate Term Loan (AED and Foreign Currency)          |\\\\n| Effective Date       | 1 March 2024                                            |\\\\n| Owner                | Head of Corporate Lending Products                      |\\\\n| Target Segment       | Corporate clients with annual turnover > AED 50 million |\\\\n| Classification       | Internal -Relationship Manager Use Only                 |\\\\n\\\\n## 1. Product Overview\\\\n\\\\nFAB\\'s Corporate Term Loan (CTL) is a bilateral lending product designed for corporate clients seeking mediumto-long-term financing for capital expenditure, project finance, acquisition finance, or general corporate purposes. The CTL is FAB\\'s primary relationship lending product in the corporate segment and is the product type most frequently processed through the GERNAS Pricing Agent.\\\\n\\\\n## 1.1 Product Parameters\\\\n\\\\n| Parameter             | Specification                                                                                            |\\\\n|-----------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Minimum Facility Size | AED 5,000,000 (or foreign currency equivalent)                                                           |\\\\n| Maximum Facility Size | Subject to single-obligor limits (FAB-CRP-CONC-2024-v1.8)                                                |\\\\n| Eligible Tenors       | 12, 24, 36, 48, 60, 72, 84 months (standard); longer tenors require GCC approval                         |\\\\n| Eligible Currencies   | AED, USD, EUR, GBP, JPY, CNY (CNY subject to treasury availability)                                      |\\\\n| Interest Rate Type    | Floating (EIBOR/SOFR + spread) or fixed rate                                                             |\\\\n| Repayment Structure   | Bullet, amortising (equal instalments), or sculpted (cash flow linked)                                   |\\\\n| Security              | Unsecured (investment grade) or secured (see Section 3)                                                  |\\\\n| Eligible Purpose      | Capex, acquisition, working capital supplement, refinancing (not margin lending or speculative purposes) |\\\\n\\\\n## 2. Eligibility Criteria\\\\n\\\\n## 2.1 Client Eligibility\\\\n\\\\nThe Corporate Term Loan is available to clients that meet all of the following eligibility criteria:\\\\n\\\\n- Incorporated and operating entity with audited financial statements for a minimum of two years.\\\\n- Annual turnover exceeding AED 50 million (or foreign currency equivalent) as evidenced by audited accounts.\\\\n- FAB internal credit rating of B or above (CCC and below are not eligible without GCC waiver).\\\\n- Clean KYC status - no outstanding AML flags or sanctions screening alerts.\\\\n- Satisfactory credit bureau check with no material adverse information within the preceding 24 months.\\\\n\\\\n## 2.2 Excluded Purposes\\\\n\\\\nThe Corporate Term Loan may not be used for the following purposes:\\\\n\\\\n- Margin lending, trading in securities, or investment in mutual funds or hedge funds.\\\\n- Financing of activities prohibited under UAE law or FAB\\'s Prohibited Sectors List (PSL-2024).\\\\n- Refinancing of existing FAB facilities at below-market pricing without proper exception approval.\\\\n\\\\n## 3. Pricing Structure\\\\n\\\\n## 3.1 Standard Pricing Components\\\\n\\\\nThe all-in pricing for a Corporate Term Loan comprises the following components, all of which must be included in the pricing model submitted for credit approval:\\\\n\\\\n| Pricing Component      | Typical Range                 | Description                                                                                                      |\\\\n|------------------------|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Base Rate (FTP)        | Set by Treasury monthly       | Matched-maturity internal cost of funds; AED EIBOR- based for AED facilities; SOFR-based for USD                 |\\\\n| Credit Spread (Margin) | 65-450 bps over FTP           | Risk-based spread reflecting client rating, security, and tenor; subject to floors in FAB-POL-PRICE-2024- v2.4   |\\\\n| Arrangement Fee        | 30-100 bps of facility amount | One-time fee payable at signing or first drawdown; included in RAROC calculation on annualised basis             |\\\\n| Commitment Fee         | 0-40 bps p.a. on undrawn      | Applicable to facilities with availability period > 30 days; standard commitment fee is 35% of applicable margin |\\\\n| Agency Fee             | USD 15,000 - 50,000 p.a.      | Applicable where FAB acts as Facility Agent in syndicated structures                                             |\\\\n\\\\n## 3.2 Pricing Floors - Reference to Credit Pricing Policy\\\\n\\\\nMinimum pricing floors applicable to all Corporate Term Loans are governed by FAB\\'s Credit Pricing Policy (FABPOL-PRICE-2024-v2.4), Section 3.1. The GERNAS Pricing Agent will automatically retrieve applicable floors from the Knowledge Graph based on the client\\'s credit rating, facility tenor, and currency. Relationship Managers must not override the floor without a documented Pricing Exception as per Section 6 of the Credit Pricing Policy.\\\\n\\\\nPRICING AGENT USERS: When using the GERNAS Pricing Agent for CTL pricing, input the following parameters to ensure correct floor retrieval: product\\\\\\\\_type = \\'corporate\\\\\\\\_term\\\\\\\\_loan\\', tenor\\\\\\\\_months = [actual months], currency = [AED/USD/etc], client\\\\\\\\_rating = [FAB internal rating]. The agent will retrieve the applicable floor from the Knowledge Graph and validate all scenarios against it.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"source\": \"FAB_Credit_Pricing_Policy_v2.4\",\\n      \"clause\": \"Section 6\",\\n      \"section\": \"FIRST ABU DHABI BANK\",\\n      \"effective_date\": \"\",\\n      \"stale\": false,\\n      \"score\": 0.0325,\\n      \"text\": \"## FIRST ABU DHABI BANK\\\\n\\\\nCommercial Banking - Credit &amp; Pricing Division\\\\n\\\\n## CREDIT PRICING POLICY\\\\n\\\\nCorporate &amp; Commercial Banking - Lending Products\\\\n\\\\n| Document Reference   | FAB-POL-PRICE-2024-v2.4                     |\\\\n|----------------------|---------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Effective Date       | 1 June 2024                                 |\\\\n| Review Date          | 31 May 2025                                 |\\\\n| Approved By          | Chief Commercial Officer, FAB               |\\\\n| Owner                | Head of Credit Pricing, Commercial Banking  |\\\\n| Classification       | STRICTLY CONFIDENTIAL -Internal Use Only    |\\\\n| Replaces             | FAB-POL-PRICE-2023-v2.3 dated 15 March 2023 |\\\\n\\\\n## 1. Purpose and Scope\\\\n\\\\nThis policy establishes the minimum pricing standards, approval authority requirements, and governance controls applicable to all credit facilities extended by First Abu Dhabi Bank (FAB) under its Corporate and Commercial Banking franchise. It applies to all relationship managers, credit officers, and product teams involved in the origination, structuring, or repricing of credit facilities.\\\\n\\\\nThis policy is designed to ensure that all credit pricing decisions:\\\\n\\\\n- Recover FAB\\'s fully loaded cost of funds, capital, and operational costs.\\\\n- Generate risk-adjusted returns commensurate with the credit risk assumed.\\\\n- Comply with applicable UAE Central Bank (CBUAE) regulations and FAB\\'s own risk appetite.\\\\n- Are documented, approved at the appropriate authority level, and auditable.\\\\n\\\\nSCOPE: This policy applies to all corporate term loans, revolving credit facilities, syndicated loans, trade finance facilities, and treasury credit products originated through FAB\\'s Corporate and Commercial Banking division. Retail lending is governed by a separate policy (FAB-POL-PRICE-RETAIL-2024).\\\\n\\\\n## 1.1 Definitions\\\\n\\\\n| Term                       | Definition                                                                                                                                                            |\\\\n|----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Floor Rate                 | The minimum all-in pricing rate for a credit facility, below which no transaction may be executed without Pricing Exception approval.                                 |\\\\n| All-In Rate                | The total annualised yield to FAB from a facility, including margin, upfront fees (annualised), commitment fees, and any other recurring fee income.                  |\\\\n| FTP (Funds Transfer Price) | The internal cost of funds allocated to each facility based on tenor, currency, and product type, as determined by FAB\\'s Treasury function on a monthly basis.        |\\\\n| RWA(Risk-Weighted Assets)  | The regulatory capital measure calculated by applying Basel III risk weights to the facility exposure, taking into account the counterparty\\'s internal credit rating. |\\\\n| Capital Cost               | The annual cost of regulatory capital consumed by a facility, calculated as RWA × FAB\\'s internal cost of equity hurdle rate.                                          |\\\\n| NIM (Net Interest Margin)  | The difference between the all-in facility rate and the applicable FTP rate, representing the gross spread earned by the business unit.                               |\\\\n| RAROC                      | Risk-Adjusted Return on Capital -the primary profitability metric used by FAB to evaluate credit transactions.                                                        |\\\\n| Relationship Value         | An adjustment to pricing that recognises the full revenue contribution of a client relationship across all products and services with FAB.                            |\\\\n\\\\n## 2. Pricing Principles\\\\n\\\\nAll credit pricing at FAB is based on four fundamental principles that must be satisfied simultaneously for a transaction to be approved:\\\\n\\\\n## 2.1 Cost Recovery\\\\n\\\\nEvery credit facility must, at a minimum, recover FAB\\'s fully loaded cost of funds (FTP), allocated capital cost, operating cost allocation, and expected credit loss provision. A transaction priced below full cost recovery may only be approved under Section 6 (Pricing Exceptions) of this policy.\\\\n\\\\nThe minimum cost recovery calculation is:\\\\n\\\\n```\\\\nMinimum Rate = FTP Rate + Capital Cost (bps) + Operating Cost Allocation (bps) + Expected Credit Loss Provision (bps)\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n## 2.2 Risk-Adjusted Profitability\\\\n\\\\nBeyond cost recovery, credit facilities must generate a RAROC that meets or exceeds FAB\\'s minimum acceptable return on equity (RoE) hurdle, as set by Group Finance and reviewed annually. The current RoE hurdle is 13.5% post-tax.\\\\n\\\\n## RAROC is calculated as:\\\\n\\\\nRAROC = (All-In Revenue - Expected Losses - Operating Costs) / Regulatory Capital\\\\n\\\\nIMPORTANT: Transactions with RAROC below the 13.5% hurdle may still be approved if they meet the Relationship Value criteria in Section 2.4 below, subject to appropriate approval authority.\\\\n\\\\n## 2.3 Market Competitiveness\\\\n\\\\nPricing must reflect prevailing market conditions for comparable transactions. Relationship Managers are required to provide market evidence (peer bank indicative pricing, publicly available benchmarks, or Bloomberg comparable data) for all transactions above AED 25 million. The Credit Pricing Committee maintains a quarterly market pricing survey that serves as the primary benchmark for this requirement.\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"source\": \"FAB_Model_Risk_Management_Framework_v3.1\",\\n      \"clause\": \"3.2\",\\n      \"section\": \"3.2 Additional MEP Components - AI/ML Pricing Models\",\\n      \"effective_date\": \"\",\\n      \"stale\": false,\\n      \"score\": 0.0317,\\n      \"text\": \"## 3.2 Additional MEP Components - AI/ML Pricing Models\\\\n\\\\nFor AI/ML systems used in credit pricing (Tier 1), the MEP must additionally include:\\\\n\\\\n8. LLM and Agent Architecture: Description of the LLM backbone, agent orchestration framework, toolcalling design, and memory architecture. Version control information for all model components.\\\\n9. Pricing Floor Compliance Mechanism: Technical description of how the model enforces pricing floor constraints. Evidence that the system is incapable of recommending a rate below the applicable floor without generating a policy exception flag.\\\\n10. Data Grounding Evidence: Documentation of the RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) architecture including: vector database configuration, document sources indexed, embedding model specifications, retrieval strategy, and re-indexing schedule.\\\\n11. Knowledge Graph Schema: For systems using a Knowledge Graph for rule lookup, documentation of the graph schema, node types, edge types, and data population procedures.\\\\n12. Explainability Artefact: A sample model output with the accompanying explanation, demonstrating that the explanation satisfies the requirements of CBUAE Circular 2024/BSE/047 Article 6.\\\\n13. Human-in-the-Loop Control Evidence: Technical description of the interrupt mechanism, approval workflow, and audit log structure. Confirmation that bypassing human approval is technically prevented.\\\\n14. Hallucination Risk Assessment: Assessment of the risk that the model may generate factually incorrect pricing information and the controls in place to detect and prevent such errors reaching end-users.\\\\n\\\\nIMPORTANT: The Data Grounding Evidence section (item 3 above) must demonstrate that all pricing floor values referenced by the model are sourced from the Knowledge Graph or RAG system, and not from the LLM\\'s parametric knowledge. Parametric knowledge is not auditable and may not reflect current policy.\\\\n\\\\n## 4. Ongoing Monitoring Requirements\\\\n\\\\n## 4.1 Performance Monitoring\\\\n\\\\nAll production AI/ML pricing models must be monitored monthly against the following metrics:\\\\n\\\\n| Metric                         | Monitoring Frequency   | Alert Threshold                                   |\\\\n|--------------------------------|------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Pricing floor breach rate      | Daily                  | Any breach triggers immediate investigation       |\\\\n| Human override rate            | Monthly                | > 20% override rate triggers model review         |\\\\n| Recommendation acceptance rate | Monthly                | < 60% acceptance rate triggers performance review |\\\\n\\\\n| RAG retrieval freshness score   | Weekly   | Average freshness < 0.80 triggers re- indexing             |\\\\n|---------------------------------|----------|------------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Knowledge Graph staleness       | Weekly   | Any node with effective_date > 6 months old without review |\\\\n| LLM response latency            | Daily    | p95 > 5 seconds triggers infrastructure review             |\\\\n| Explanation completeness score  | Monthly  | < 90% complete explanations triggers model review          |\\\\n\\\\n## 5. Incident Reporting\\\\n\\\\nA model incident is defined as any event in which an AI/ML model produces an output that: (a) breaches a pricing floor; (b) results in customer detriment; (c) triggers a regulatory notification; or (d) results in a financial loss attributable to model error.\\\\n\\\\nAll model incidents must be reported to the Model Risk Management team within 24 hours of detection, and to the CBUAE within 30 calendar days where the incident is material (threshold: financial impact &gt; AED 1 million or regulatory breach).\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \"source\": \"CBUAE_Circular_2024_BSE_047_AI_Governance\",\\n      \"clause\": \"Article 1.4\",\\n      \"section\": \"CENTRAL BANK OF THE UNITED ARAB EMIRATES\",\\n      \"effective_date\": \"\",\\n      \"stale\": false,\\n      \"score\": 0.0315,\\n      \"text\": \"## CENTRAL BANK OF THE UNITED ARAB EMIRATES\\\\n\\\\nBanking Supervision &amp; Examination Department\\\\n\\\\n## SUPERVISORY CIRCULAR\\\\n\\\\n## No. 2024/BSE/047\\\\n\\\\n## Governance and Risk Management Requirements for Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning Systems in Licensed Financial Institutions\\\\n\\\\n| Circular Reference   | CBUAE/BSE/2024/047                                                                                 |\\\\n|----------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\\\\n| Issue Date           | 15 March 2024                                                                                      |\\\\n| Effective Date       | 1 July 2024                                                                                        |\\\\n| Addressed To         | Chief Executive Officers and Chief Risk Officers of all Licensed Financial Institutions in the UAE |\\\\n| Subject              | AI/ML Governance, Model Risk Management, and Consumer Protection Requirements                      |\\\\n| Supersedes           | CBUAE/BSE/2022/031 (Guidance on Automated Decision-Making)                                         |\\\\n| Compliance Deadline  | 31 December 2024 for Tier 1 institutions; 30 June 2025 for all others                              |\\\\n\\\\n## Article 1 - Purpose, Scope and Legal Basis\\\\n\\\\n1.1 This Circular is issued pursuant to the powers vested in the Central Bank of the UAE under Federal DecreeLaw No. 14 of 2018 regarding the Central Bank and Organisation of Financial Institutions and Activities, as amended, and the accompanying Executive Regulations. 1.2 This Circular establishes minimum standards for the governance, development, deployment, monitoring, and decommissioning of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) systems used by Licensed Financial Institutions (LFIs) in the UAE in connection with credit decisions, risk management, pricing of financial products, customer service, fraud detection, and any other material business function. 1.3 This Circular applies to all LFIs licensed and supervised by the Central Bank of the UAE, including but not limited to national banks, foreign bank branches, finance companies, exchange houses, and payment service providers, to the extent that they deploy AI or ML systems in material business processes. 1.4 For the purposes of this Circular, an AI/ML system is considered to be deployed in a \\'material business process\\' where its outputs directly or indirectly influence decisions that: (a) affect a customer\\'s access to or terms of a financial product or service; (b) generate recommendations that are presented to employees or customers as a basis for financial action; or (c) are used to calculate, estimate, or propose prices, rates, fees, or other commercial terms for financial products.\\\\n\\\\nIMPORTANT: The pricing of credit facilities, including the use of AI-generated pricing recommendations for corporate loans, revolving facilities, or any structured credit product, is explicitly classified as a material business process under Article 1.4(c). All requirements in this Circular apply to such systems.\\\\n\\\\n## Article 2 - Governance Framework Requirements\\\\n\\\\n## 2.1 Board and Senior Management Accountability\\\\n\\\\n2.1.1 The Board of Directors of every LFI deploying AI/ML systems in material business processes must adopt and maintain a written AI Governance Policy. This policy must be reviewed and re-approved by the Board at least annually. 2.1.2 A designated member of senior management, at a level no lower than Chief Risk Officer or equivalent, must be appointed as the AI Risk Officer responsible for overseeing the implementation of this Circular. 2.1.3 The AI Governance Policy must address, at minimum: (a) the LFI\\'s risk appetite for AI-related risks; (b) accountability structures for AI development and deployment; (c) human oversight requirements; (d) model\\\\n\\\\ninventory management; (e) consumer protection obligations; and (f) regulatory reporting obligations.\\\\n\\\\n## 2.2 AI Model Inventory\\\\n\\\\n2.2.1 Every LFI must maintain a centralised, up-to-date AI Model Inventory covering all AI/ML systems deployed in material business processes. The inventory must be made available to the Central Bank upon request.\\\\n\\\\n2.2.2 For each model in the inventory, the following minimum information must be recorded:\\\\n\\\\n- Model name, version, and unique identifier.\\\\n- Business function and processes in which the model is deployed.\\\\n- Date of initial deployment and date of most recent material update.\\\\n- Model owner (business function) and model developer (technology/data team).\\\\n- Validation status and date of last independent validation.\\\\n- Current monitoring status (active / under review / decommissioned).\\\\n- Risk classification (High / Medium / Low) as determined under Article 4.\\\\n\\\\n## Article 3 - Model Risk Management\\\\n\\\\n## 3.1 Model Development Standards\\\\n\\\\n3.1.1 All AI/ML models deployed in material business processes must be developed in accordance with sound model development practices, including but not limited to: clearly documented model objectives; appropriate training data governance; out-of-time and out-of-sample testing; and documentation of model limitations and known failure modes.\\\\n\\\\n3.1.2 Training data used for models that influence credit pricing or credit decisions must be sourced from reliable, auditable data systems. Data lineage must be documented from source to model input.\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \"total_results\": 4,\\n  \"freshness_warning\": false,\\n  \"latency_ms\": 183.2\\n}'}]}"}
{"ts": "2026-07-03T13:49:44+0530", "level": "INFO", "logger": "agent_framework", "trace_id": "66f49fa6d04025f90916ef3b3abf70f7", "span_id": "a6e8a011107867a1", "parent_span_id": "-", "request_id": "9DE1C0B2", "msg": "{'role': 'assistant', 'parts': [{'type': 'text', 'content': 'Based on FAB\\'s current Credit Pricing Policy and Corporate Term Loan Product Manual, the loan pricing methodology is a risk-adjusted, cost-recovery model designed to ensure pricing meets the bank’s profitability hurdles while remaining competitive.\\n\\nHere are the standard components and calculation steps for determining a recommended loan price:\\n\\n### 1. Pricing Components ([Source: `FAB_Corporate_Term_Loan_Product_Manual_v2.0` · Section 3.1])\\nThe \"All-In\" recommended price consists of both the interest margin and annualized fee income. The standard components include:\\n\\n*   **Base Rate (FTP):** The internal Funds Transfer Price set by Treasury monthly based on matched-maturity cost of funds (e.g., EIBOR plus spread for AED; SOFR plus spread for USD).\\n*   **Credit Spread (Margin):** A risk-based premium over the FTP (typically ranging from 65 to 450 bps) reflecting the client\\'s internal credit rating, security, and tenor.\\n*   **Arrangement Fee:** A one-time upfront fee (typically 30–100 bps of the facility amount) which is annualized for profitability calculations.\\n*   **Commitment Fee:** An annualized fee on undrawn amounts (standard is 35% of the applicable margin).\\n*   **Agency Fee (Optional):** Applicable only if FAB acts as a Facility Agent in syndicated loans.\\n\\n### 2. Calculation Steps for Recommended Price ([Source: `FAB_Credit_Pricing_Policy_v2.4` · Sections 2.1, 2.2])\\n\\nThe pricing calculation follows a structured hierarchy to ensure cost coverage and return targets are met:\\n\\n**Step 1: Establish the Minimum Cost Recovery Rate**\\nBefore any profit margin is added, the price must cover FAB\\'s fully loaded costs. \\n*   **Formula:** `Minimum Rate = FTP Rate + Capital Cost + Operating Cost Allocation + Expected Credit Loss (ECL) Provision`\\n\\n**Step 2: Determine Risk-Adjusted Return on Capital (RAROC)**\\nThe primary profitability metric used to set the final recommendation is RAROC. The total price must generate a RAROC that meets or exceeds FAB’s minimum post-tax Return on Equity (RoE) hurdle (currently 13.5%).\\n*   **Formula:** `RAROC = (All-In Revenue - Expected Losses - Operating Costs) / Regulatory Capital`\\n\\n**Step 3: Formulate the All-In Rate**\\nThe final recommended price aggregates the margin, the annualized upfront arrangement fee, and the ongoing commitment fee to reach the RAROC target.\\n\\n**Step 4: Validate Against Pricing Floors & Market**\\n*   **Floor Check:** The recommended price is cross-referenced against hard pricing floors in the Knowledge Graph (based on rating, tenor, and currency). RMs cannot override these floors without a documented Pricing Exception. \\n*   **Market Check:** For transactions exceeding AED 25 million, the price must be aligned with peer bank indications or market benchmarks (as per `FAB Credit Pricing Policy` Section 2.3).'}]}"}
